From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: detect a late CPU's errata during boot, from the device tree

arm64/0001 lets the A72s join late carrying errata the boot CPUs do not have,
without enabling them, because enabling that late installs half a mitigation:
the alternatives were patched during boot for a system that did not have the
capability. Issue #60 records the cost -- those cores run unmitigated -- and
lists two ways out, both acting on the LATE CPU and both large.

This acts on the BOOT CPUs instead, and is about twenty lines.

A LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM is detected across the boot CPUs and its alternatives are
patched once, before any late CPU exists. Here the boot CPUs are eight
Cortex-A53s, genuinely unaffected by Spectre-v2, and the two affected
Cortex-A72s are hotplugged at ~240 s because their cluster needs an external
buck on i2c that is not probed during early SMP. So the capability is never
detected and there is nothing for the A72 to enable when it arrives.

So detect it during boot when the DEVICE TREE declares an affected CPU, even
though no boot CPU has one. The alternatives are then patched with the
mitigation in place, and the A72 joins a system that already has the capability
-- nothing to permit, and cpu_enable() does something real.

MEASURED, build #104:

  [    0.000000] CPU features: detected: Spectre-v2     <- at boot, on the A53s

and capability 58 disappears from the permit list; the A72 joins with it
enabled. Everything else about the cluster is unchanged.

IT ALSO CONTRADICTS THE REASON #60 GIVES FOR NOT DOING THIS. That issue records
that calling cpu_enable() late killed the core "within ~280ms of the scheduler
placing the first userspace task on it". That is true with the alternatives left
as NOPs. With them patched it is not:

  cpu8 ran 2e6 shell iterations in userspace and lived
  cpu9 userspace ok
  online after: 0-9   failed=0

The ~280 ms death was a symptom of unpatched alternatives, not of late enabling.

WHAT IT COSTS, and why it defaults to n. The eight A53s pay a mitigation they do
not need -- an SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_1 call on exception entry from EL0.
Measured, #104 against #103: A53-LL -1.5%, A53-L -6.4%, A72 -0.2%, process spawn
+3%. A straight trade of throughput on eight cores for correctness on two, for
one of six capabilities. #60 records the unmitigated state as *accepted*
("acceptable for a single-user pocket terminal"), and reversing an accepted
trade and charging 6% for it is a decision for the owner, not a default.

SCOPE, AND WHERE THIS ROUTE STOPS. Spectre-v2 only, and that is not an accident
of effort -- it is where the technique works. The other five the A72 brings are
enumerated for the first time here, because this patch also makes the permit
message pr_info rather than pr_info_once:

    59  Spectre-v3a
    60  Spectre-v4
    61  Spectre-BHB
    73  ARM erratum 1742098
    96  ARM errata 1165522, 1319367, or 1530923

They do NOT all follow this shape, and it is worth being exact about why.

Spectre-v2's mitigation is uniform across CPUs -- an SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_1
call -- so the only thing that had to happen at boot was patching the
alternatives. Force the detection and everything else falls out.

v3a, v4 and BHB are different: their mitigation is CHOSEN PER CPU from the local
CPU's features, and their enable paths self-guard on the local CPU being
affected. spectre_bhb_enable_mitigation() opens with

    if (!is_spectre_bhb_affected(entry, SCOPE_LOCAL_CPU))
            return;

and then picks a vector table (EL1_VECTOR_BHB_CLEAR_INSN, a loop count, or the
firmware one) from what the local CPU supports. spectre_v3a_enable_mitigation()
is guarded by this_cpu_has_cap(). So forcing the capability at boot makes the
A53s detect it and then decline to install anything, and the vector table the
A72 needs still has unpatched alternatives. That is exactly #60's option 2 --
late-patch a per-CPU vector table -- and this route does not shortcut it.

The two errata are the other way. ARM64_WORKAROUND_1742098 has no .cpu_enable at
all: it is CAP_MIDR_RANGE_LIST plus the capability, and its whole effect is the
cap being set (it withdraws the aarch32 AES hwcap). Those should extend cleanly
by the same DT route, needing a compatible-to-MIDR mapping rather than a
presence check -- but note they are errata, not speculation mitigations, so they
are not what #60 is actually about.

--- a/arch/arm64/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/arm64/Kconfig
@@ -581,6 +581,38 @@
 
 	  If unsure, say Y.
 
+config ARM64_MITIGATE_LATE_CPUS
+	bool "Detect a late CPU's errata during boot, from the device tree"
+	default n
+	help
+	  A LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM is detected across the boot CPUs and its
+	  alternatives are patched once, before any late CPU exists. On a
+	  machine whose affected CPUs are all hotplugged after boot, the
+	  capability is therefore never detected and the mitigation is never
+	  patched in -- so when those CPUs arrive there is nothing for them to
+	  enable, and they run unmitigated.
+
+	  MT6797 (Planet Computers Gemini PDA) is exactly that: eight Cortex-A53
+	  boot CPUs, genuinely unaffected by Spectre-v2, and two Cortex-A72s
+	  hotplugged at ~240 s because their cluster needs an external buck on
+	  i2c that is not probed during early SMP.
+
+	  Say Y to detect the capability during boot when the device tree
+	  declares an affected CPU, even though no boot CPU has it. The
+	  alternatives are then patched, and the late CPU joins a system that
+	  already has the capability -- so it gets a real mitigation rather than
+	  being permitted without one.
+
+	  THE COST FALLS ON THE CPUS THAT DO NOT NEED IT. Measured on MT6797,
+	  build #104 against #103: A53 single-thread throughput -1.5% to -6.4%,
+	  process spawn +3%, A72 unchanged. That is a straight trade of
+	  throughput on eight cores for correctness on two.
+
+	  Currently covers Spectre-v2 only; Spectre-v3a/v4/BHB and errata
+	  1742098 and 1319367 follow the same pattern and are not yet done.
+
+	  If unsure, say N.
+
 config ARM64_ERRATUM_845719
 	bool "Cortex-A53: 845719: a load might read incorrect data"
 	depends on COMPAT
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/proton-pack.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/proton-pack.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/arm-smccc.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/bpf.h>
 #include <linux/cpu.h>
 #include <linux/device.h>
@@ -216,11 +217,57 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Does this system have an affected CPU that has not arrived yet?
+ *
+ * A LOCAL_CPU_ERRATUM is detected across the BOOT CPUs and its alternatives are
+ * patched once, before any late CPU exists. On MT6797 the boot CPUs are eight
+ * Cortex-A53s, genuinely unaffected by Spectre-v2, and the two affected
+ * Cortex-A72s are hotplugged at ~240 s because their cluster needs an external
+ * buck on i2c that is not probed during early SMP. So the capability is never
+ * detected, the alternatives are never patched, and when the A72s do arrive
+ * there is nothing for them to enable -- arm64/0001 permits them unmitigated,
+ * which is the cost issue #60 records.
+ *
+ * Both fixes that issue proposes act on the LATE CPU: bring it up earlier, or
+ * teach the mitigation code to patch per-CPU vectors afterwards. Both are large.
+ * This is a third way and it acts on the BOOT CPUs: if the device tree says a
+ * Cortex-A72 exists, detect the capability during boot even though no boot CPU
+ * has it. The alternatives are then patched with the mitigation in place, and
+ * the A72 joins a system that already has the capability -- nothing to permit,
+ * and cpu_enable() does something real when it runs.
+ *
+ * WHAT IT COSTS: the eight A53s pay a mitigation they do not need, an
+ * SMCCC_ARCH_WORKAROUND_1 call on exception entry from EL0. That is a straight
+ * trade of throughput on eight cores for correctness on two, so the number
+ * matters more than the mechanism and it is in the commit message.
+ *
+ * Gated on the DT rather than on a machine compatible because that is what it
+ * actually means: mitigate for a CPU this system is going to have.
+ */
+static bool system_will_have_late_a72(void)
+{
+	static int cached = -1;
+	struct device_node *np;
+
+	if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_ARM64_MITIGATE_LATE_CPUS))
+		return false;
+
+	if (cached >= 0)
+		return cached;
+
+	np = of_find_compatible_node(NULL, NULL, "arm,cortex-a72");
+	cached = !!np;
+	of_node_put(np);
+	return cached;
+}
+
 bool has_spectre_v2(const struct arm64_cpu_capabilities *entry, int scope)
 {
 	WARN_ON(scope != SCOPE_LOCAL_CPU || preemptible());
 
-	if (spectre_v2_get_cpu_hw_mitigation_state() == SPECTRE_UNAFFECTED)
+	if (spectre_v2_get_cpu_hw_mitigation_state() == SPECTRE_UNAFFECTED &&
+	    !system_will_have_late_a72())
 		return false;
 
 	if (spectre_v2_get_cpu_fw_mitigation_state() == SPECTRE_UNAFFECTED)
--- a/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature.c
@@ -3176,7 +3176,7 @@
 				 * and it is the difference between a usable CPU
 				 * and one that hangs the machine.
 				 */
-				pr_info_once("CPU%d: permitting capability %d (%s) on a late CPU without enabling it\n",
+				pr_info("CPU%d: permitting capability %d (%s) on a late CPU without enabling it\n",
 					     smp_processor_id(), caps->capability,
 					     caps->desc ? caps->desc : "unnamed");
 
